-- ── Drag-mode input blocker ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- The scattered `if guiDragMode then return end` checks inside each
-- widget only cover the specific handlers someone remembered to guard
-- (Toggle/Slider/Dropdown/Button/Input) — anything else (ColorPicker,
-- Keybind, dropdown list items, the slider's own lock button, tab
-- buttons, dynamically-created JoinGroup/Config buttons, etc.) still
-- receives input normally, which is why elements were still reachable
-- while unlocked.
--
-- This overlay is the actual fix: a single invisible Frame, parented
-- directly under mainFrame (so it sits above tabFrame AND contentArea
-- and therefore above every tab/groupbox/popup nested inside them,
-- regardless of that content's own internal ZIndex), covering the
-- entire window. `Active = true` is what makes a Frame participate in
-- input hit-testing at all — Frames default to Active = false, which
-- is why an inert overlay would never have blocked anything even if
-- one existed. While Visible, this overlay is hit FIRST for every
-- click/tap over the window, so the button/toggle/slider/etc. beneath
-- it never receives the input in the first place — not "receives it
-- and ignores it", it simply never arrives.
-- BackgroundTransparency stays 1 at all times, so nothing about the
-- window's appearance changes when this toggles.
-- The window-drag itself is unaffected: dragging is driven by the
-- separate UserInputService.InputBegan/Changed/Ended listener below,
-- which reads raw input events directly and doesn't depend on GUI hit
-- testing, so it keeps working whether or not this overlay is up.
local dragBlocker = Make("Frame", {
    Name                   = "DragBlocker",
    Size                   = UDim2.fromScale(1, 1),
    Position               = UDim2.fromScale(0, 0),
    BackgroundTransparency = 1,
    BorderSizePixel        = 0,
    ZIndex                 = 10000,
    Visible                = false,
    Active                 = true,
}, mainFrame)

